{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790717051,"nonce":"ri8jkhx1QokGJjUCOeq8qnEBkHA=","message":"75f9dc07ab5e840e5d13658043d96224af1a4545 adds a compact snapshot interface for lowered modules. Named imports now decode the non-function interface plus only the requested complete function declarations, preserving overloads and original declaration order; the full TypedProgram stays encoded. Lua and Python also read this interface when they only need the library type registry.\n\nNUC x86_64, LLVM 21, `any_field`, 31 interleaved process runs:\n\n- prior selective import: 27.781 ms median wall (25.614..32.644)\n- compact interface: 26.521 ms (23.576..30.660), 4.5% lower\n- both print `result: 1500000`\n\nThe builtins frontend payload falls from 162,975 bytes decoded in 2.08 ms to a 2,287-byte interface decoded in 0.11 ms. The traced library phase falls from 3.10 ms to 0.22 ms. HIR and the 182 adopted bodies are unchanged, so they remain the next part of this issue.\n\nValidation: 13 snapshot tests pass; both Lua and Python check with `llvm-backend`; the NUC release conformance run passes 41/43 non-official targets including `load`, stdlib, C API, and stressed GC. The two failures are the existing `gc/weak_value_keys.lua` result (`string keys 6` versus pinned `5`), reproduced identically five times with both the prior and new binaries. The long binary-reader case also completes correctly with the new binary.","files":null}]}